    "candidate_text": "Let\n\\[\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\qquad t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\qquad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\n\\]\nWe claim that, for every \\(k\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2.\n\\]\nThis holds initially, and\n\\[\n(2t+3y)^2-3(t+2y)^2=t^2-3y^2,\n\\]\nso it is preserved by the recurrence. Moreover, \\(t_k,y_k\\) are positive odd integers: this is true initially, and the recurrence preserves positivity and oddness. Also\n\\(y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k\\), so the \\(y_k\\) are strictly increasing.\n\nDefine\n\\[\nn_k=\\frac{y_k+1}{2},\\qquad m_k=\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}.\n\\]\nBoth are positive integers, since \\(y_k\\) and \\(t_k\\) are odd. From \\(y_k=2n_k-1\\) and \\(t_k^2-3y_k^2=-2\\), we obtain\n\\[\nt_k^2=3(2n_k-1)^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\n\\]\nThus \\(t_k^2\\) is the discriminant of\n\\[\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+(n_k^2+n_k)=0,\n\\]\nand the quadratic formula shows that \\(m_k\\) is a root. Hence\n\\[\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k-4m_kn_k=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(m_k,n_k>0\\), division by \\(m_kn_k\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\n\\]\na positive integer. Finally, the strictly increasing values of \\(y_k\\), and therefore of \\(n_k\\), make the pairs \\((m_k,n_k)\\) distinct. Consequently there are infinitely many such pairs of positive integers.",
